\label{ch:hm-inference}

The Hindley--Milner (HM) type system represents one of the most successful marriages of theory and practice in programming language design. It provides parametric polymorphism with full type inference---the programmer writes no type annotations, yet every expression receives the most general type possible. First articulated by Hindley~\cite{hindley1969} in the context of combinatory logic and independently rediscovered by Milner~\cite{milner1978} for the ML language, the system remains the foundation of type inference in Haskell, OCaml, F\#, Rust (for its local inference), and many other languages.

This chapter presents the HM type system in depth. We begin with the two classic inference algorithms---Algorithm~W and Algorithm~J---then develop the theory of principal types and let-polymorphism. We proceed to extensions that have shaped modern type systems: type classes, qualified types, and multi-parameter type classes. We close with implementation techniques for production-quality type inference engines.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 44.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Algorithm W}\label{sec:algorithm-w}

Algorithm~W is the original type inference procedure for the HM system. It is a syntax-directed algorithm that traverses the expression tree, maintaining an environment mapping variables to type schemes and a substitution accumulating the constraints discovered so far.

\subsection{Type Schemes and the Let-Rule}\label{sec:type-schemes}

The key innovation of the HM system is the notion of a \emph{type scheme}, which generalizes a type by universally quantifying over its free variables that are not free in the environment.

\begin{definition}[Type Scheme]\label{def:type-scheme}
A \emph{type scheme} (or \emph{polytype}) is a type of the form $\forall \alpha_1 \ldots \alpha_n.\, \tau$, where $\tau$ is a monotype (a type containing no quantifiers). The \emph{generalization} of a type $\tau$ with respect to an environment $\Gamma$ is:
\[
  \mathrm{gen}(\Gamma, \tau) = \forall \vec{\alpha}.\, \tau \quad \text{where } \vec{\alpha} = \mathrm{fv}(\tau) \setminus \mathrm{fv}(\Gamma)
\]
\end{definition}

The let-rule uses generalization to assign polymorphic types to let-bound variables:

\begin{semrules}{HM Type Schemes}
  \typerule{Gen}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau \quad \vec{\alpha} = \mathrm{fv}(\tau) \setminus \mathrm{fv}(\Gamma)}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \forall \vec{\alpha}.\, \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Inst}{\Gamma \vdash e : \forall \vec{\alpha}.\, \tau \quad \tau' = \tau[\vec{\beta}/\vec{\alpha}]}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau'}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{The Algorithm}\label{sec:algorithm-w-def}

Algorithm~W is defined as a function $\mathcal{W}(\Gamma, e) = (\sigma, \tau)$ returning a substitution $\sigma$ and a monotype $\tau$ such that $\sigma(\Gamma) \vdash e : \tau$.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Algorithm W}\label{alg:algorithm-w}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{$\mathcal{W}$}{$\Gamma, e$}
  \State \textbf{case} $e$ \textbf{of}
  \Statex
  \State \quad $x$: \quad
    \Let{\sigma}{\mathrm{instantiate}(\Gamma(x))}
    \State \quad \Return $(\sigma, \sigma(\Gamma(x)))$
  \Statex
  \State \quad $\lambda x.\, e'$: \quad
    \Let{\alpha}{\mathrm{fresh}()}
    \State \quad $(\sigma_1, \tau_1) \gets \mathcal{W}(\Gamma[x \mapsto \alpha], e')$
    \State \quad \Return $(\sigma_1, \sigma_1(\alpha) \to \tau_1)$
  \Statex
  \State \quad $e_1\; e_2$: \quad
    \Let{(\sigma_1, \tau_1)}{\mathcal{W}(\Gamma, e_1)}
    \Let{(\sigma_2, \tau_2)}{\mathcal{W}(\sigma_1(\Gamma), e_2)}
    \Let{\sigma_3}{\mathrm{unify}(\sigma_2(\tau_1), \tau_2 \to \alpha)} \quad \text{where } \alpha \text{ fresh}
    \State \quad \Return $(\sigma_3 \circ \sigma_2 \circ \sigma_1, \sigma_3(\alpha))$
  \Statex
  \State \quad $\mathtt{let}\; x = e_1\; \mathtt{in}\; e_2$: \quad
    \Let{(\sigma_1, \tau_1)}{\mathcal{W}(\Gamma, e_1)}
    \Let{\sigma}{\sigma_1}
    \Let{\Gamma'}{\sigma_1(\Gamma)}
    \Let{\sigma'}{\text{generalize } \tau_1 \text{ over } \Gamma'}
    \State \quad \Return $\mathcal{W}(\Gamma'[x \mapsto \mathrm{gen}(\Gamma', \tau_1)], e_2)$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Correctness of Algorithm W}\label{sec:algorithm-w-correctness}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of Algorithm W]\label{thm:alg-w-sound}
If $\mathcal{W}(\Gamma, e) = (\sigma, \tau)$, then $\sigma(\Gamma) \vdash e : \tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness of Algorithm W]\label{thm:alg-w-complete}
If $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$, then there exists $(\sigma, \tau')$ such that $\mathcal{W}(\Gamma, e) = (\sigma, \tau')$ and a substitution $\rho$ such that $\tau = \rho(\tau')$ and $\sigma(\Gamma) = \rho(\sigma(\Gamma))$.
\end{theorem}

Together, these theorems establish that Algorithm~W computes a \emph{principal pair}: the most general typing of an expression.

\subsection{Walk-Through Example}\label{sec:walk-through}

\begin{example}[Inferring the type of the identity combinator applied to itself]\label{ex:ww}
Consider $e = (\lambda x.\, x)\; (\lambda y.\, y)$.

\textbf{Step 1.} Infer the type of $\lambda x.\, x$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Fresh variable $\alpha$ for $x$.
  \item $\mathcal{W}(\{x : \alpha\}, x) = (\mathrm{id}, \alpha)$.
  \item Result: $(\mathrm{id}, \alpha \to \alpha)$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2.} Infer the type of $\lambda y.\, y$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Fresh variable $\beta$ for $y$.
  \item $\mathcal{W}(\{y : \beta\}, y) = (\mathrm{id}, \beta)$.
  \item Result: $(\mathrm{id}, \beta \to \beta)$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3.} Unify the function type with the argument type:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Unify $\alpha \to \alpha$ with $(\beta \to \beta) \to \gamma$.
  \item Result: $\sigma = \{\alpha \mapsto \beta \to \beta, \gamma \mapsto \beta \to \beta\}$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Final type:} $\beta \to \beta$ (the polymorphic identity type).
\end{example}

