\section{Algorithm J}\label{sec:algorithm-j}

Algorithm~J, presented by Jouvelot and Gifford~\cite{jouvelot1988}, reformulates Algorithm~W in a constraint-based style. Rather than threading a substitution through the computation, Algorithm~J accumulates a set of type equations and solves them at the end.

\subsection{The Constraint Accumulation Style}\label{sec:constraint-style}

Algorithm~J defines a judgment $\Gamma \vdash_J e : \tau \mid C$ meaning ``expression $e$ has type $\tau$ under environment $\Gamma$, subject to constraint set $C$.''

\begin{semrules}{Algorithm J}
  \typeaxiom{Var}{\Gamma, x : \sigma \vdash_J x : \mathrm{instantiate}(\sigma) \mid \emptyset} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{\Gamma, x : \alpha \vdash_J e : \tau \mid C \quad \alpha \text{ fresh}}
            {\Gamma \vdash_J \lambda x.\, e : \alpha \to \tau \mid C} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{\Gamma \vdash_J e_1 : \tau_1 \mid C_1 \quad
                  \Gamma \vdash_J e_2 : \tau_2 \mid C_2 \quad
                  \alpha \text{ fresh}}
            {\Gamma \vdash_J e_1\; e_2 : \alpha \mid C_1 \cup C_2 \cup \{\tau_1 = \tau_2 \to \alpha\}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Let}{\Gamma \vdash_J e_1 : \tau_1 \mid C_1 \quad
                  \Gamma, x : \mathrm{gen}(\Gamma, \tau_1) \vdash_J e_2 : \tau_2 \mid C_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash_J \mathtt{let}\; x = e_1\; \mathtt{in}\; e_2 : \tau_2 \mid C_1 \cup C_2}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Relationship Between W and J}\label{sec:w-vs-j}

\begin{theorem}[Equivalence of W and J]\label{thm:w-j-equiv}
$\mathcal{W}(\Gamma, e) = (\sigma, \tau)$ if and only if $\Gamma \vdash_J e : \tau' \mid C$ and $\sigma$ is the MGU of $C$ and $\tau = \sigma(\tau')$.
\end{theorem}

Algorithm~J makes the constraint structure explicit, which is advantageous for:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Error reporting.} Constraints carry source locations, enabling precise error messages.
  \item \textbf{Extension.} New constraint forms (type classes, functional dependencies) can be added without restructuring the algorithm.
  \item \textbf{Optimization.} Constraints can be simplified, reordered, or solved incrementally.
\end{itemize}

