\section{Let-Polymorphism}\label{sec:let-polymorphism}

The let-rule is the heart of HM's polymorphism. It allows a let-bound variable to be used at multiple, unrelated types within its scope---a feature called \emph{let-polymorphism} or \emph{ML-polymorphism}.

\subsection{The Value Restriction}\label{sec:value-restriction}

The standard let-rule generalizes over all type variables not free in the environment. However, this is unsound in the presence of mutable references. Consider:

\begin{verbatim}
  let r = ref [] in
    r := [1];
    hd (!r) + true   (* type error: hd (!r) is int, but + true expects int *)
\end{verbatim}

If $r$ were given the polymorphic type $\forall \alpha.\, \mathrm{ref}\; [\alpha]$, we could instantiate it as $\mathrm{ref}\; [\mathrm{int}]$ for the first assignment and $\mathrm{ref}\; [\mathrm{bool}]$ for the second, breaking type safety.

\begin{definition}[Value Restriction]\label{def:value-restriction}
The \emph{value restriction} (Wright~\cite{wright1995}) restricts generalization to expressions that are syntactic values:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Variables: $x$
  \item Abstractions: $\lambda x.\, e$
  \item Constructor applications: $C\; v_1 \ldots v_n$ where each $v_i$ is a value
  \item Let-bindings: $\mathtt{let}\; x = v_1\; \mathtt{in}\; v_2$ where $v_1, v_2$ are values
\end{itemize}
Only these expressions may be generalized at a let-binding.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Monomorphism Restriction}\label{sec:monomorphism-restriction}

Haskell uses a different approach called the \emph{monomorphism restriction}: a binding without an explicit type signature is monomorphic if it is a ``pattern binding'' (not a simple variable binding with a function-like right-hand side). This is less principled than the value restriction but handles common cases more gracefully.

\subsection{Polymorphic Recursion}\label{sec:polymorphic-recursion}

HM cannot infer types for functions that call themselves at different types:

\begin{verbatim}
  let rec f = function
    | [] -> []
    | x :: xs -> f (map length xs)  (* f applied at different type *)
  in f
\end{verbatim}

Here, $f$ has type $\forall \alpha.\, [\alpha] \to [\mathrm{int}]$ (roughly), but the recursive call uses $f$ at type $[[\alpha]] \to [[\mathrm{int}]]$. Inferring this requires solving a fixed-point equation over types, which is undecidable in general~\cite{kfoury1999}.

\begin{insight}
Polymorphic recursion is decidable if the programmer provides a type signature. Languages like Haskell and OCaml require explicit signatures for polymorphically recursive functions. The undecidability result of Kfoury and Tiuryn shows that this restriction is not merely an implementation limitation but a fundamental boundary of the HM system.
\end{insight}

