\section{Principal Types}\label{sec:principal-types-hm}

The existence of principal types is the crowning achievement of the HM type system. It guarantees that the inference algorithm finds the ``best possible'' type for every expression.

\subsection{The Principal Type Theorem}\label{sec:principal-type-thm}

\begin{theorem}[Principal Type Theorem for HM]\label{thm:principal-hm}
For every expression $e$ and environment $\Gamma$, if there exists any type $\tau$ such that $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$, then Algorithm~W computes a type $\tau_0$ such that for all such $\tau$, there exists a substitution $\sigma$ with $\sigma(\tau_0) = \tau$ (up to generic instantiation).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The proof proceeds by induction on the structure of $e$. The key insight is that unification computes the most general solution to a set of type equations. At each step, Algorithm~W computes the most general typing of the subexpression, and the let-rule preserves this generality by generalizing only over variables not constrained by the environment. The composition of MGUs at each step yields the overall MGU. \qed
\end{proof}

\subsection{Consequences}\label{sec:principal-consequences}

The principal type property has profound consequences:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{No annotations needed.} The programmer never needs to provide type information; the compiler infers the most general type.
  \item \textbf{Deterministic.} The inferred type is unique (up to renaming), so there is no ambiguity about what a program means.
  \item \textbf{Modular.} Each function's type can be computed independently and used as a contract for its callers.
  \item \textbf{Composable.} The principal type of a composite expression is determined by the principal types of its parts.
\end{enumerate}

