\section{Type Inference with Type Classes}\label{sec:type-classes}

Type classes, introduced by Wadler and Blott~\cite{wadler1989}, extend HM with \emph{ad hoc} polymorphism: the ability to define functions that behave differently at different types, constrained by a set of type-class membership requirements.

\subsection{Qualified Types}\label{sec:qualified-types}

\begin{definition}[Qualified Type]\label{def:qualified-type}
A \emph{qualified type} has the form $C \Rightarrow \tau$, where $C$ is a set of \emph{constraints} (predicates of the form $K\; \tau$ for a type class $K$ and type $\tau$) and $\tau$ is a monotype. The qualified type $C \Rightarrow \tau$ denotes the type of expressions that have type $\tau$ provided that all constraints in $C$ are satisfied.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[The Eq type class]\label{ex:eq-class}
\begin{verbatim}
  class Eq a where
    (==) :: a -> a -> Bool

  instance Eq Int where
    x == y = primEqInt x y

  instance Eq a => Eq [a] where
    []     == []     = True
    (x:xs) == (y:ys) = (x == y) && (xs == ys)
    _      == _      = False
\end{verbatim}
The function $\mathtt{elem} :: \mathrm{Eq}\; a \Rightarrow a \to [a] \to \mathrm{Bool}$ has a qualified type: it requires its first argument's type to be an instance of $\mathrm{Eq}$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Inference with Constraints}\label{sec:inference-constraints}

Type inference with type classes extends Algorithm~W by accumulating type-class constraints alongside type equations. The extended judgment is:
\[
  \Gamma \vdash e : \tau \mid C
\]
where $C$ is now a set of both type equations and type-class constraints.

The constraint-solving phase must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Solve type equations by unification (as before).
  \item Resolve type-class constraints by finding instance declarations that match.
  \item Verify that all constraints are satisfiable (the \emph{constraint entailment} problem).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{semrules}{Type Class Inference}
  \typerule{Class-App}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 : C_1 \Rightarrow \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \mid E_1 \quad
                        \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_1 \mid E_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e_1\; e_2 : \tau_2 \mid C_1 \cup E_1 \cup E_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Class-Gen}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau \mid C \quad
                        \vec{\alpha} = \mathrm{fv}(C, \tau) \setminus \mathrm{fv}(\Gamma)}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : (\vec{K}\;\vec{\alpha}) \Rightarrow \tau \mid C}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Ambiguity and Defaulting}\label{sec:ambiguity-defaulting}

A qualified type is \emph{ambiguous} if a quantified variable appears in the constraints but not in the body type:
\[
  \forall a.\, \mathrm{Num}\; a \Rightarrow \mathrm{Int}
\]
This type is ambiguous because $a$ is not determined by the result type. Haskell resolves this through \emph{defaulting rules}: ambiguous numeric constraints are defaulted to $\mathrm{Integer}$ (or the first type in the default list that satisfies the constraints).

