\section{Extensions}\label{sec:hm-extensions}

\subsection{Multi-Parameter Type Classes}\label{sec:mptc}

Multi-parameter type classes (MPTCs) allow type classes to relate multiple types:

\begin{verbatim}
  class Collection c a where
    empty  :: c
    insert :: a -> c -> c
    member :: a -> c -> Bool
\end{verbatim}

Here, $\mathrm{Collection}\; c\; a$ relates a collection type $c$ with its element type $a$. MPTCs introduce the \emph{ambiguity problem}: the type of $\mathtt{empty}$ is $\mathrm{Collection}\; c\; a \Rightarrow c$, but neither $c$ nor $a$ is determined by the result.

\subsection{Functional Dependencies}\label{sec:fun-deps}

Functional dependencies (Jones~\cite{jones2000}) constrain the relationship between type parameters:

\begin{verbatim}
  class Collection c a | c -> a where
    empty  :: c
    insert :: a -> c -> c
\end{verbatim}

The dependency $c \to a$ states that the element type $a$ is determined by the collection type $c$. This eliminates ambiguity and improves type inference by allowing the solver to deduce $a$ from $c$.

\subsection{Associated Type Families}\label{sec:associated-types}

Associated type families (Chakravarty et al.~\cite{chakravarty2005}) allow type-level functions to be defined within type classes:

\begin{verbatim}
  class Iterable c where
    type Elem c
    next :: c -> Maybe (Elem c, c)
\end{verbatim}

The associated type $\mathrm{Elem}\; c$ computes the element type of a collection $c$. Type inference with type families requires solving type-level equations, which can be undecidable in general.

\subsection{Constraint Kinds}\label{sec:constraint-kinds}

GHC's constraint kinds extension allows constraints to be manipulated as first-class entities:

\begin{verbatim}
  type EqShow a = (Eq a, Show a)
  f :: EqShow a => a -> String
\end{verbatim}

This enables abstraction over constraint sets, supporting patterns like constraint synonym families and quantified constraints.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Comparison of type class extensions}\label{tab:tc-extensions}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Inference} & \textbf{Decidable} & \textbf{GHC} & \textbf{OCaml} \\
\hline
Single-parameter classes & Full & Yes & Yes & Yes (modules) \\
Multi-parameter classes & Limited & Yes & Yes & Yes (functors) \\
Functional dependencies & Improved & Yes & Yes & N/A \\
Type families & Limited & No (in general) & Yes & N/A \\
Constraint kinds & Full & Yes & Yes & N/A \\
Quantified constraints & Full & Yes & Yes & N/A \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

