\section{Implementation: Type Inference Engines}\label{sec:inference-engines}

Building a production-quality type inference engine requires careful attention to data structures, performance, and error reporting.

\subsection{Union-Find for Type Variables}\label{sec:union-find}

The most critical data structure in a type inference engine is the union-find (disjoint-set) structure for representing type variable equivalence classes.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Union-Find with Path Compression and Union by Rank}\label{alg:union-find}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{type} $\mathrm{Node} = \{ \mathrm{parent} : \mathrm{Node}, \mathrm{rank} : \mathrm{Int}, \mathrm{type} : \mathrm{Type} \}$
\Statex
\Function{Find}{$x$}
  \If{$x.\mathrm{parent} \neq x$}
    \State $x.\mathrm{parent} \gets \mathrm{Find}(x.\mathrm{parent})$ \Comment{Path compression}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $x.\mathrm{parent}$
\EndFunction
\Statex
\Function{Unify}{$x, y$}
  \Let{rx}{\mathrm{Find}(x)}
  \Let{ry}{\mathrm{Find}(y)}
  \If{$rx = ry$}
    \State \Return $\mathrm{ok}$
  \EndIf
  \If{$rx.\mathrm{rank} < ry.\mathrm{rank}$}
    \State $rx.\mathrm{parent} \gets ry$
  \ElsIf{$rx.\mathrm{rank} > ry.\mathrm{rank}$}
    \State $ry.\mathrm{parent} \gets rx$
  \Else
    \State $ry.\mathrm{parent} \gets rx$
    \State $rx.\mathrm{rank} \gets rx.\mathrm{rank} + 1$
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $\mathrm{ok}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Lazy Substitution Application}\label{sec:lazy-substitution}

Rather than applying substitutions eagerly to all types in the environment, modern implementations use \emph{lazy} substitution: types are represented as mutable cells that are updated in place when a unification occurs. This reduces the cost of substitution from $O(n)$ per variable to $O(1)$ amortized per unification step.

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Generalizing Let Bindings with Metavariable Levels}

Algorithm~W subtracts the free variables of the environment when
generalizing an inferred type. Repeatedly scanning a large environment
is avoidable in a pure HM implementation. Give each fresh unbound
metavariable the nesting level of its inference scope; infer the
right-hand side of a nonrecursive let at level $\ell+1$ and
generalize at its enclosing level $\ell$. A metavariable can escape
to an older scope through unification, so a binding of an unbound
variable $a$ to a type $t$ must first lower all unbound variables
reachable through $t$ to at most the level of $a$. The routine
assumes that the ordinary unifier has already dereferenced both sides
and checked that $a$ does not occur in $t$.

\begin{sudocode}
\Procedure{Link}{$a,t$}
  \ForAll{unbound roots $b$ reachable from $t$}
    \State $\mathrm{lev}[b]\gets\min(\mathrm{lev}[b],\mathrm{lev}[a])$
  \EndFor
  \State link $a$ to $t$ \Comment{Lower before publishing the link}
\EndProcedure
\Function{Generalize}{$t,\ell$}
  \State $Q\gets\varnothing$
  \ForAll{unbound roots $a$ reachable from $t$}
    \If{$\mathrm{lev}[a]>\ell$}
      \State insert $a$ in $Q$ \Comment{Not reachable from the old scope}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return a scheme binding exactly $Q$ in $t$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

At level 0 under \texttt{fun x ->}, let \texttt{x} have type $a$.
In \texttt{let f = fun y -> x y}, inference introduces argument and
result variables $b,c$ at level 1. Applying \texttt{x} links $a$ to
$b\to c$, lowering both variables to level 0. The inferred type of
\texttt{f} is $b\to c$, but the generalizer binds neither variable.
By contrast, the fresh argument variable in
\texttt{let id = fun z -> z} stays at level 1 and is quantified.

\Cref{fig:visual-44} shows the link that makes the initially fresh
variables in \texttt{f} reachable from the enclosing scope.

