\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-44}

The Hindley--Milner type system demonstrates that powerful polymorphism and complete type inference can coexist. Algorithm~W and Algorithm~J provide two perspectives on the same fundamental computation: the constraint-based view of Algorithm~J has proven more extensible, while Algorithm~W remains elegant in its directness.

The extensions to HM---type classes, functional dependencies, type families---have transformed it from a simple polymorphic type system into a rich framework for expressing and enforcing program invariants. The implementation techniques---union-find, lazy substitution, interleaved constraint solving---have been refined over decades of practical use.

In the next chapter, we move beyond HM to System~F, the polymorphic lambda calculus, which provides the theoretical foundation for parametric polymorphism and serves as the target language for compiling type classes and other forms of ad hoc polymorphism.
